omitted: 31
weights:
  - "broadside_dpm 12 (HP/min, higher is better): sustained damage against one target at the best bearing: tubes bearing, reload and damage together"
  - "broadside_alpha 10 (HP, higher is better): damage of one launch at one target, every torpedo hitting"
  - "torpedoes_per_broadside 8 (torpedoes, higher is better): torpedoes in one spread at one target; a wider, denser spread is harder to dodge"
  - "damage 8 (HP, higher is better): damage of each hit"
  - "damage_at_max_range 6 (HP, higher is better): damage of a hit at the end of the run; torpedoes with damage falloff lose it on long launches"
  - "reload 6 (s, lower is better): how often the launchers fire"
  - "reaction_time 10 (s, lower is better): time a target has to turn away after spotting the torpedo, the main limit on hits"
  - "speed 7 (kn, higher is better): time to the target, in which it can leave the spread"
  - "range 7 (km, higher is better): launch distance, which lets a launch come from beyond detection"
  - "detectability 3 (km, lower is better): distance at which ships spot the torpedo; most of its effect is already in reaction_time"
  - "flood_coefficient 3 (x, higher is better): flooding chance of a hit, damage on top of the hit"
  - "firing_arc 4 (deg, higher is better): degrees of one side, out of 179, at which a launcher fires; a narrow arc needs a precise turn to launch"
  - "bow_dead_zone 3 (deg, lower is better): angle off the bow inside which no launcher fires; a small one launches while closing bow-in"
  - "stern_dead_zone 3 (deg, lower is better): angle off the stern inside which no launcher fires; a small one launches while retreating"
  - "targets 10 (% of classes, higher is better): share of the five ship classes the torpedo can hit; the score loses this weight times the share it cannot hit, as deep-water torpedoes pass under destroyers and submarines"
rule: "Classes considered: destroyers. Each torpedo option (a torpedo a ship can carry; a ship with alternative torpedoes has one option per torpedo) ranks within the group on each factor. A factor's standing is pNN, the share of the other options it beats in the direction better for that factor, ties counting half. score = sum of weight x standing over the factors the option has other than targets / sum of those weights, minus the targets weight x the share of the five ship classes the torpedo cannot hit (4 for a deep-water torpedo hitting cruisers, battleships and carriers; 6 for one hitting only battleships and carriers), from 0 to 100, higher better. Lower is better for reload, reaction_time, detectability, bow_dead_zone and stern_dead_zone: a torpedo spotted only from 0.6 km is seen later than one spotted from 1.5 km, a strength. reaction_time = detectability / speed. Values are on each ship's best-fit build for each factor, so speed may assume Torpedo Acceleration while range does not. torpedoes_per_broadside, broadside_alpha (torpedoes_per_broadside x damage), firing_arc and the dead zones come from each launcher's firing arcs on the hull, on the module combination carrying the torpedo with the most tubes bearing at one bearing on either broadside; a centerline launcher counts on both sides. firing_arc counts the bearings of one side at which any launcher fires; the dead zones are the smallest angles off the bow and off the stern at which any launcher fires. broadside_dpm is the damage per minute of the launchers bearing on one target at the bearing where the most tubes bear, on its own best-fit build; it is left out where the launcher facings are unknown. Damage figures assume every torpedo hits. strengths and weaknesses name up to three factors each, those moving the score most, among standings more than 10 points above or below the middle of the group (p50; for targets, below 90); broadside_alpha is not named beside torpedoes_per_broadside, which it follows from, nor damage_at_max_range beside an equal damage, and the dead zones are not named strengths when firing_arc is in the narrowest quarter of the group. The score is a judgment from game data and these weights, not win rates or hit rates: say so when quoting it, and explain a rank from strengths and weaknesses."
